Am 22.02.13 10:44, schrieb Heiko Hund:
> Add the option --verify-x509-name to provide the functionality
> of the now deprecated --tls-remote.
>
> The new option accepts RFC 2253 subject DNs only and compares
> RDN or RDN prefix only if configured explicitly.
>
I looked through these and I think they are okay. Since most users don't=20
know what RDN, RDN prefix and DN are an example like

A certificate with a DN CN=3Dopenvpn.example.com, OU=3DAvian IP Carriers,=20
L=3DNRW would be matched by:

verify-x509-name "C=3DDE, OU=3DBLINKT, CN=3Dopenvpn.blinkt.de"

or

verify-x509-name openvpn.blinkt.de subject

or

verify-x509-name openvpn.bl name-prefix


Most user and certificates will probably use the second form with=20
subject. I also think that for standard x509 certificates it may be a=20
good option to also include name-suffix so you can do:

verify-x509-name .blinkt.de name-suffix


+        || (opt->verify_x509_type =3D=3D VERIFY_X509_SUBJECT_RDN
+            && strcmp (opt->verify_x509_name, common_name) =3D=3D 0)

+        || (opt->verify_x509_type =3D=3D VERIFY_X509_SUBJECT_RDN_PREFIX
+            && strncmp (opt->verify_x509_name, common_name,
+                        strlen (opt->verify_x509_name)) =3D=3D 0) )

This is correct but it took me a while to understand that strcmp will=20
compare the 0 btye in opt-verify_x509_name and strncmp with strln as=20
length will not include the 0. The joys of C...

Arne


